      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+4?$",
      "worked_solution": "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228213/assets/d67114a9865ca54cdf14798e779cfa9c00e71598ba652d6fccdcf686f86e20ef.png)\n\n \n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228213/assets/d6dcae76f7fe89188ef449c817f7e7af48c25763077d1daee9626d2f26b1a78a.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $4$ units up to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}+4.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228213/assets/529dfc1ef1bc6f0ff58addca6cf27c5039a6cf18c5a307c06a153a3d56040ede.png)",
